# Git attributes for ourobion.
#
# Deliberately minimal: one path, one reason. Add entries only with a stated
# reason, because attributes silently change how git diffs, merges and checks
# out files.

# ─── supabase/functions/compute-baselines/index.ts ────────────────────────────
# This file contains a DELIBERATE literal NUL (0x00) byte: it is the separator
# in the `${user_id}\x00${metric_key}` baseline map key. See the file itself and
# the repo's Windows-toolchain notes, which call out that this NUL is intentional
# and must be preserved (an accidental NUL elsewhere is a known write-tool bug,
# but this one is load-bearing).
#
# Because git classifies a file as binary when it finds a NUL in the first 8000
# bytes, `git diff --numstat` reports `-  -  <path>` for it instead of line
# counts. The Run 4 landing gate (tools/run4_release_gate.mjs, `checkLandingDelta`)
# fails closed on exactly that row — "binary/unparsable diff row" — because a
# binary diff cannot be measured against the added-line cap. That is correct
# behaviour for a real binary, but here it is a false positive on a TypeScript
# source file, and it blocks any unit that edits this file (R4-U2 was the first).
#
#   -text  never apply EOL conversion, so the stored and checked-out bytes stay
#          byte-identical. This matters beyond tidiness: supabase/deploy-attestation.json
#          pins this file's entrypointSha256, so any EOL rewrite would invalidate
#          the recorded attestation.
#   diff   treat it as text when diffing, so --numstat yields real line counts
#          and the landing cap can measure it.
#
# Neither attribute alters the blob's content; they only change how git presents
# and converts it.
supabase/functions/compute-baselines/index.ts -text diff

# The before-blob has legacy NUL separators; force this transition's text diff
# so numstat and landing caps can inspect its actual line delta.
tools/brain-ingest/src/synth/artifact.ts -text diff

# ─── model-training/evidence/*/local-bundle-sha256sums.txt ────────────────────
# These two files are CONTENT-ADDRESSED: each private R2 release is named
# `sha256-<hex>` where the hex is the SHA-256 of the file itself
# (model-training/src/ourobion_model_lab/inference/releases.py re-derives it and
# fails closed on a mismatch). Their bytes are therefore load-bearing, not just
# their lines.
#
# On the repo's Windows-native dev machine `core.autocrlf=true` checks them out
# as CRLF, which changes the digest:
#
#   zebra-v1    LF e1d09fbd…  ->  CRLF e3806b3b…
#   viceroy-v0  LF 751fbf1f…  ->  CRLF 45cbf55c…
#
# Neither CRLF digest matches the pinned release id, so every inference run and
# the tracked-pin tests fail on Windows while passing on Linux CI. `text eol=lf`
# forces the working-tree bytes to LF on every platform, which is what the
# release ids were taken over.
model-training/evidence/*/local-bundle-sha256sums.txt text eol=lf

# ─── model-training/inference-manifests/*.jsonl ───────────────────────────────
# Frozen research inputs. Not currently hashed, but they must be byte-identical
# across platforms so a prediction file is reproducible from the same manifest
# on any machine — and so they stay hashable if a pin is added later.
model-training/inference-manifests/*.jsonl text eol=lf
